// EFB-68 — the project that actually enforces "worker code gets worker types".
//
// ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `tsconfig.json` pins `types` to @cloudflare/workers-types, and that pin was
// believed to keep node's type surface out of `src/`. It does not, and never
// did. Two separate reasons, both verified rather than reasoned about:
//
//   1. The `types` array only gates AUTOMATIC GLOBAL inclusion. A `node:`
//      module import resolves through @types/node regardless, and once any
//      file in a program pulls @types/node in, its global declarations —
//      `process`, `Buffer`, `ImportMeta.url` — are visible to EVERY file in
//      that program, `src/` included.
//
//   2. It does not even take a `node:` import to trigger it. The root project
//      includes `tests/**/*.ts`, every test imports `vitest`, and vitest's own
//      type surface transitively pulls @types/node. So the pin has been open
//      since the first test file entered the program — long before any test
//      imported a node builtin directly.
//
// Measured, by varying only the root `include` and probing with
// `process.env["X"]` inside `src/slug.ts`:
//
//   include: src/** only                     -> TS2591, `process` not found
//   include: src/** + tests/slug.test.ts     -> compiles clean
//   include: full, minus the node-using test -> compiles clean
//
// `tests/slug.test.ts` imports nothing but `vitest` and the module under test.
//
// The reason nobody noticed is that `src/` happens not to use `process` or
// `fs` today. That is a coincidence, not a defence — and the failure it was
// protecting against is a bad one: node built-ins typecheck fine and then
// throw at runtime on the Workers runtime, so the mistake surfaces in
// production rather than in the editor.
//
// ── WHAT THIS PROJECT IS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The same compiler options as the root, over `src/` ALONE. No tests, so no
// vitest, so no @types/node, so `process` and `node:fs` genuinely fail to
// resolve — which is the invariant the comments elsewhere have been claiming
// all along.
//
// Run it with `npm run typecheck:src`. It is a separate script from
// `typecheck` rather than chained onto it because `typecheck` exits non-zero
// on pre-existing errors, so an `&&` chain would never reach this project —
// the same reasoning tsconfig.integration.json records for its own script.
//
// This project is the enforcement. Keep it that way: do not add `tests/**` to
// the include below to make some test typecheck, because that single edit
// silently restores the hole this file exists to close.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  // `exclude` is restated because it is inherited and the base excludes
  // directories that are already outside this project's `include`.
  "exclude": ["node_modules", ".wrangler"],
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "src/**/*.tsx", "worker-configuration.d.ts"]
}
